Synthetic SULTAN campaign cache
==============================

Provenance
----------
Every file here is synthesised. No SULTAN measurement is included, and none was
available to copy from: the shipped data directory is empty, and no `.dat` or
campaign workbook has ever been committed to this repository's history. The
content was written to match the file format the readers in
`nova/thermalhydralic/sultan/` implement, at the smallest size that still drives
every parsing branch. It is therefore safe to redistribute -- there is nothing
proprietary in it -- and it carries no measurement anyone should cite.

The numbers are a plausible helium-cooled conductor test, not a validated one:
5.5 bar inlet at 4.5 K, 8 g/s mass flow, a 230 A pulse coil driving a sinusoidal
field between t = 1.5 s and t = 5.5 s, and an outlet temperature that rises
through a single exponential during the pulse and relaxes through another
afterwards. That shape is chosen so the heat index finds one driven window, the
profile finds one maximum, and the cooldown threshold has something monotone to
bisect.

Layout
------
The tests point `XDG_DATA_HOME` at a copy of this directory, so `Sultan/` below
it is what `appdirs.user_data_dir(appname="Sultan")` resolves to and `ftp/` is
the source sub-directory `LocalData` downloads into.

    SYNTH_1/ftp/SYNTH_1_testplan.xlsx   campaign metadata workbook
    SYNTH_1/ftp/SYNTH1A110611.dat       ac shot, 1 Hz, 150 samples
    SYNTH_1/ftp/SYNTH1A110612.dat       ac shot, 2 Hz, 120 samples

The two ac shots differ in length so that advancing the shot index is visible in
the profile, which is what pins the reload propagation. The workbook also
declares a two-shot dc phase; it ships no timeseries because no test reads one,
and it exists to give the mode split and the phase index two entries.

Workbook structure
------------------
The reader takes column A as the campaign's spine. Row 0 holds the strand
template `SYNTH1-AXXYYZZ`, from which the strand prefix `SYNTH1` is derived --
the reader strips the `XXYYZZ` placeholder, drops the test-mode letter and
removes hyphens, so the template must stand alone on its row. Each phase is then
a section heading, a two-row column header, and one row per shot:

    SYNTH1-AXXYYZZ
    (blank)
    AC Loss Initial
    File            B Sultan  I Sample  P. Freq  I pulse  Notes
                    T         kA        Hz       A
    SYNTH1A110611   10.8      45.0      1.0      230 A    1 Hz sweep
    SYNTH1A110612   10.8      45.0      2.0      230 A    2 Hz sweep
    DC Test
    File            B Sultan  I Sample  P. Freq  I pulse  Notes
                    T         kA        Hz       A
    SYNTH1D110621   10.8      45.0      0.0      230 A    dc hold
    SYNTH1D110622   10.8      60.0      0.0      230 A    dc hold

A shot's test mode is the character of its filename at the strand's length, so
`SYNTH1A110611` is an ac shot and `SYNTH1D110621` a dc one. The header names are
the pre-rename spellings (`B Sultan`, `I Sample`, `P. Freq`, `I pulse`) so the
column-renaming branch is exercised rather than bypassed.

Timeseries structure
--------------------
Each `.dat` is comma-separated with a single header row, which is what
`SultanData._read_data` reads. The columns are the ones the sample conditioning
selects by name:

    Time                        s
    dm/dt left, dm/dt right     g/s, scaled to kg/s on read
    PS EEI (I)                  pulse-coil current, A
    T11..T14, T21..T24          left and right inlet rakes, K
    T31..T34, T41..T44          left and right outlet rakes, K
    P in left,  P out left      bar, scaled to Pa on read
    P in right, P out right     bar

Side names are written lower case and `T11` carries a ` (320K)` suffix so the
reader's column-normalising branches -- capitalising the side, collapsing double
spaces, stripping the calibration suffix -- all run. Four probes per rake are
required because the conditioning averages them.

Unverified assumption
---------------------
The comma delimiter is what `SultanData._read_data` asks for
(`pandas.read_csv` with its default separator, ISO-8859-1 encoding) and has been
so throughout this repository's history. It has not been checked against a file
produced by the SULTAN facility, because none is available here. If a real
export turns out to be tab- or whitespace-separated, the reader is what is wrong
and this fixture will need to follow it.
